course: dictionary
lesson: 16058
title: Seeing it vividly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Vividly means very clearly and in a lively way, so you can almost see, feel or remember something. «I vividly remember my first day at school.» You can almost picture that classroom again, so vividly tells you the memory is clear and strong.
explain: Writers use vividly when they want readers to picture a scene. «The market was vividly described, with noisy sellers and stalls piled high with fruit.» The writer helps you see the market, hear the sellers and smell the fruit.
explain: Vividly often comes before a word about colour: «The poison dart frog is vividly coloured.» To check, swap in clearly and brightly. If the sentence still makes sense, vividly fits.
fact: Vividly means very clearly and in a lively way.
fact: «Vividly coloured» means coloured with very bright, strong colours.
fact: Vividly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done, remembered or described.
fact: Poison dart frogs are vividly coloured, and their bright colours warn predators to stay away.
fact: Good writers use strong verbs, adjectives and the senses to describe a scene vividly.
exercise: «I vividly remember my first day at school.» What does vividly mean here? | Sadly, with tears | *Very clearly, so it feels like I can see it again | Not at all, so I forget it | Very quickly, in a hurry
exercise: What does «vividly coloured» mean? | *Coloured with very bright, strong colours | Coloured with one dull shade of grey | Coloured in pencil only | Hidden under leaves so nobody can see it
exercise: «She vividly described the storm.» Which word means about the same as vividly here? | quietly | slowly | badly | *clearly
test: «The old film shows the city vividly.» What does vividly show about the city? | *Clearly and in lively detail | Only in a dim, blurry way | Without any colour, in every scene | Very quickly, so you cannot follow it
test: Why might a writer use vividly in a story? | To show that something happened long ago | To turn a sentence into a question | To make the sentence shorter | *To help the reader picture the scene clearly
exam: Lila says, «The sunset was vividly painted across the sky.» What does this tell you? | The sunset was painted with a pencil and no colour | The sunset happened very quickly and was over | The sky was grey and cloudy all evening | *The sky was painted in bright, strong colours
